WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:04.900
Despite doing well with critics and having
barely gotten started with its first season,

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.100
Swamp Thing has gotten the axe at DC Universe.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.610
From debunked rumors to behind-the-scenes
drama, here's a look at everything that led

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.240
to this comics adaptation's quick cancellation.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:17.840
The first sign that the series was stumbling
came even before the first episode aired on

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.300
DC Universe in late-May, 2019.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.260
In mid-April it was reported production on
Swamp Thing was being shut down early and

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:28.680
that the show's first season, originally planned
for 13 episodes, was being whittled down to

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:29.180
10.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.920
There was no word on exactly why Swamp Thing's
first season was cut short, but the news certainly

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.370
didn't boost fan confidence.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.769
Neither did a now-deleted Instagram post from
one of the series' cast members.

00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:42.800
Virginia Madsen, who played the troubled Maria
Sunderland in Swamp Thing, wrote on her Instagram

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:43.300
page:

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.460
"I'm beyond sad.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:45.700
What a terrible decision.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.260
We have been cut to the core by those who
have never set foot into the Swamp."

00:00:49.269 --> 00:00:54.089
Even executive producer James Wan admitted
to being baffled by the decision to sink Swamp

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:57.289
Thing, although he still encourages fans to
check out the first season.

00:00:57.289 --> 00:00:58.880
In an Instagram post, Wan wrote:

00:00:59.049 --> 00:01:03.199
"Don’t really know or understand why #Swampthing
was cancelled, but I can tell you this all

00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:07.180
the cast and crew, and producing/writing team
poured their hearts into this.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.700
Really proud of everyone’s hard work.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.760
Go watch episode 2, and immortalize these
10 episodes.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:12.760
Swampy deserves it."

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.260
One of the earliest and most widespread speculations
about the show's sudden cancelation was made

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.300
by Movies.com writer John Gholson in a series
of now-deleted tweets.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.890
The story, which has since been debunked,
is that Swamp Thing was cut short because

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.970
of an accounting error blamed on the state
of North Carolina, where the series was filming.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.710
"It's probably just an accounting error.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:34.140
It is not an accounting error.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:35.860
Well, it could be.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.480
It is not an accounting error."

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:42.360
The story went that Swamp Thing's budget was
$80 million and that North Carolina through

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.460
a program meant to encourage filmmaking in
the state had promised to cover $40 million

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.560
of the production costs through state grants.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.870
Then, according to Gholson's story, someone
in the North Carolina government messed up

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:57.680
and Swamp Thing's production was only granted
$12 million of the promised $40 million.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.980
So, short $28 million, the Swamp Thing production
closed up shop.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:02.900
"Okay, my bad.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:03.400
Shut it down.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:04.820
Shut it down!

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:05.620
That's it."

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:10.580
But shortly after Gholson's story began to
circulate, ScreenRant reported that his accounting

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.120
error theory simply wasn't true.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.920
Swamp Thing was granted $12 million for its
first season's production.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:20.440
They were also allowed a separate grant of
$4.9 million for the pilot episode.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.320
There was never a discussion of North Carolina
picking up half the bill for Swamp Thing.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:29.160
According to some, the cancellation of Swamp
Thing doesn't only impact that series, but

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.720
could threaten every other original DC Universe
show.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.720
In June 2018, AT&amp;T completed its acquisition
of Time Warner, of which the DC Universe streaming

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:38.860
service is a part.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:44.020
When Deadline reported Swamp Thing's cancellation,
the outlet pointed out that, quote "DC Universe

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.620
is being reevaluated following the completion
of AT&amp;T acquisition."

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.830
The implication seems to be that if WarnerMedia
is going to have its own streaming service,

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:55.690
a separate DC Universe streaming platform
might be considered redundant.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:59.860
If that's the case, then producing new original
series in the interim could be considered

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:00.360
a waste.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:06.300
"Fudge."

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.420
Commenting on the cancellation of Swamp Thing
on the horror website BloodyDisgusting, writer

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.770
Brad Miska speculated that Swamp Thing had
become, quote, "the first of probably many

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:16.560
scapegoats."

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:21.140
The problem, Miska wrote, is that DC Universe
was experiencing problems and something had

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:21.640
to give.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:26.440
Comparing it to AMC's handling of the horror
streaming service Shudder, he criticized DC

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:27.880
Universe for giving up too soon.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.600
Miska certainly could be right.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.620
Perhaps suffering in Marvel's shadow on the
big screen has made Time Warner executives

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:37.480
more skittish in the face of anything less
than complete and undeniable success.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.240
With some time having passed since Swamp Thing's
cancellation, it now seems like the series'

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.930
premature ending could be chalked up to the
oldest showbiz roadblock of all:

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.400
"Apparently they've got some real creative
differences."

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:52.819
The Playlist reported that the call to shorten
Swamp Thing was a creative decision, and not

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:58.930
part of a reevaluation of DC Universe's performance,
which they said was, quote, "outpacing expectations."

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:03.240
SlashFilm likewise reported the season truncation
was due to "creative reasons."

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:07.140
One of the few sites to offer any specifics
in their update was BloodyDisgusting, who

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:10.940
claim their source told them the creative
differences in question were about the nature

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:11.700
of the series.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:15.540
According to the site, some people behind
the scenes wanted Swamp Thing to be a horror

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:16.040
series…

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.940
"It was very important for all of us that
it be sort of, that it be grounded more in

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:20.940
the horror genre."

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.300
...while others wanted a "weekly procedural."

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:26.930
We're not sure how anyone pictures a swamp
monster might be able to dust crime scenes

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:28.600
for fingerprints — but okay!

